# Phase 1 task cards

Each card is the complete brief for one parallel agent. Shared rules,
layout and import mapping: [PLAN.md](../PLAN.md). Row IDs refer to
[patch-inventory.md](../patch-inventory.md); deviations:
[DEVIATIONS.md](../DEVIATIONS.md).

Common to every card:
- Work only in your own git worktree/branch of this repository; commit there
  with clear messages ending with the `Co-Authored-By` line given by the
  orchestrator. Do not merge into `main`.
- Implement exactly your rows. If an inventory equivalence argument for a
  narrow hook fails, use the listed fallback REPLACE and report it.
- Every `@patch`/`@attach` passes `row="<inventory row>"`. Activation
  requires the declarations of a feature to equal `manifest.json` exactly
  (generated from the inventory by the orchestrator; do not edit it). If a
  row needs a different hook than listed (e.g. an equivalence fallback),
  implement it, and report the change; the orchestrator updates the
  inventory and manifest at merge.
- Fingerprints: `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=99 <validation python> tools/fingerprint.py
  write <your-module> <targets...>` for every target and `depends` name,
  against the pin only. Records include CPU binding chains; the orchestrator
  adds CUDA chains before GPU validation. `depends` entries must be functions
  or classes (fields and instance attributes are not definitions). Properties
  cannot be hook targets.
- Run `tools/run_cpu_tests.sh` (all tests, including the framework suite).
  Until every row of a feature is implemented, real activation of that
  feature fails by design; test your hooks by activating against a manifest
  restricted to your rows (see `tests/test_framework.py` for the pattern).
  A ported test that needs another workstream's patches stays unchanged,
  gets `@pytest.mark.integration`, and is listed in your report; the
  orchestrator runs those after merging.
- Prohibited actions: PLAN.md rule 6. Use `-k 'not ServiceLifecycleTests'`
  when running fork service tests (rule 8).
# W1 runtime

Rows: N01, N03, N04 (N02 code is moved; its behavior belongs to W6).

The fork runtime package is already in `src/sglang_qsa_hisparse/hisparse/`
(verbatim, imports rewritten). Your job is to prove it.

1. Port the fork tests `test/qsa_hisparse/test_runtime.py`, `test_slots.py`,
   `test_single_request.py`, `test_gather.py` into `tests/runtime/` with only
   import-path changes (mapping in PLAN.md). Keep every assertion.
2. Where a test reaches SGLang behavior that the fork changed (e.g. FP8
   descale kernels, `QSATokenToKVPool(full_kv_pool=...)`, scheduler hooks),
   do not reimplement it: mark the test `integration` and name the inventory
   rows it needs.
3. Add `tests/runtime/test_moved_sources.py`: for every moved module, assert
   that its text equals the fork file at `ee8fe158d6` after applying exactly
   the documented import rewrites (read the fork with `git show`).
4. Document in `docs/runtime.md` every private SGLang definition the runtime
   relies on (e.g. `mamba_pool._slot_siblings`, `qsa_rope_position_buffer`,
   `ReqToTokenPool.req_generation`) with the pinned definition providing it.
   Put their dotted names in `RUNTIME_DEPENDS` in a new module
   `src/sglang_qsa_hisparse/hisparse/depends.py` (moved modules stay
   verbatim) and pin them with `tools/fingerprint.py write runtime ...`.
   W4's runtime-construction hook (row Q03) passes `depends=RUNTIME_DEPENDS`,
   so drift in any of them fails activation. Entries must be functions or
   classes; for an instance attribute, name the method that creates it.

Owned paths: `src/sglang_qsa_hisparse/hisparse/` (except W6's prefix behavior),
`tests/runtime/`, `docs/runtime.md`, `src/sglang_qsa_hisparse/fingerprints/runtime.json`
(`hisparse/depends.py` included).
# W2 scheduler and lifecycle

Rows: S01–S09, B01–B06, P01, P02, M01–M04 (S01, B06, P01, P02, M01, M03 are
the host-prefix call sites W6 verifies; you implement them).

Fork files: `managers/scheduler.py`, `managers/schedule_policy.py`,
`managers/scheduler_components/{batch_result_processor,weight_updater}.py`,
`mem_cache/{allocation,common,memory_pool}.py`, `mem_cache/allocator/paged.py`.

1. Implement each row in `src/sglang_qsa_hisparse/patches/hisparse/scheduler.py`,
   `patches/hisparse/lifecycle.py`, and (for model_compat rows such as M04)
   `patches/model_compat/lifecycle.py`, with fingerprints in matching
   `fingerprints/<module>.json`. Generic model_compat rows obey the
   target-model scope (PLAN.md rule 9; call
   `sglang_qsa_hisparse.scope.target_model_active()`, monkeypatched in tests).
2. Preserve the release order exactly: prefix capture/`before_release` →
   runtime release (drain GPU and copy events) → `cache_finished_req`
   (logical free inside the free group) → `req_to_token_pool.free` →
   `mark_kv_released` → `after_release(lease)` → allocator free-group end →
   `after_logical_flush` (physical slot reuse). Add
   `tests/lifecycle/test_release_order.py` that records the call order with
   independent fakes for each path (finish, abort, rollback of a failed
   restore, flush) and asserts it, plus a negative test that a physical slot
   is never reusable before the logical flush.
3. Port the fork's edits to the pinned upstream tests it touched
   (`test_batch_result_processor_hidden_states.py`,
   `test_scheduler_chunked_req_gate.py`) as plugin tests run with the hisparse
   patches active; the pinned originals must still pass with the plugin off.
4. With the plugin off, every hooked function must be the pinned original
   (test via `inspect.unwrap` identity after a no-switch `plugin.load()`).

Owned paths: `patches/*/scheduler.py`, `patches/*/lifecycle.py`, their
fingerprint files, `tests/lifecycle/`.
